{"reason": "The initial plan aims to improve DistilBERT by replacing with RoBERTa, increasing max length, tuning learning rates, and adding regularization. Historical experiments show that progressive unfreezing and deep fusion of stylometric features successfully mitigate overfitting (experience #1), while swapping meta-learner to XGBoost stacking reduces overfitting (experience #2). The execution results confirm DistilBERT overfits after epoch 2 (val loss increases from 0.4250 to 0.4874) and the ensemble achieves 0.3607 log loss. To address overfitting and strengthen the transformer, I refine the plan to three modules: model_design (progressive unfreezing + stylometric fusion), training_evaluation (adjusted scheduling and early stopping), and data_processing_and_feature_engineering (stronger augmentation). This retains the initial plan's core of architecture upgrade and optimizer tuning, while incorporating successful historical strategies to improve robustness.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Replace current weak augmentation with an enhanced pipeline: (a) random word swaps within 3-word windows at 10% probability, (b) random punctuation insertion ('--', ';', ':', '...') at sentence boundaries at 15% probability, (c) character-level vowel noise (replace 5% of vowels with random vowels), (d) keep synonym replacement at 5% probability. WHY: The current overfitting (val loss rising after epoch 2) indicates insufficient regularization. Historical experiment #1 shows stronger augmentation improves generalization. These augmentations target author-specific stylistic markers (punctuation for Poe, vowel changes for Lovecraft). HOW: Modify the augment_text function to apply exactly one random augmentation per sample with the specified probabilities. Interface: function signature unchanged (input text, output augmented text).", "model_design": "WHAT: Two changes: (1) Implement progressive unfreezing: first 2 epochs only train classifier and pooling, then unfreeze top 4 RoBERTa layers every 2 epochs. (2) Add a parallel stylometric encoder (3-layer MLP: 37->64->32->16 with LayerNorm and ReLU) that takes engineered features and concatenates its output (16-dim) with RoBERTa pooled (512-dim) before classifier. WHY: The initial plan's RoBERTa replacement benefits from capacity control via progressive unfreezing, as shown in historical experiment #1. Deep fusion of stylometric features directly into the network provides strong discriminative signals the transformer struggles to extract from limited data. HOW: Replace DistilBertClassifier with a new class using 'roberta-base'. Add stylometric_encoder submodule. In forward, accept optional 'stylometric_features' tensor, concatenate with pooled output. Modify training loop to freeze/unfreeze layers based on epoch.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement progressive unfreezing schedule: after 2 epochs, unfreeze top 4 layers; after 4 epochs, next 4; etc. Use separate learning rates: 5e-5 for newly unfrozen layers, 5e-6 for previously unfrozen lower layers, 1e-4 for classifier/pooling/stylometric_encoder. Use linear decay scheduler (LambdaLR). Increase weight decay to 0.05. Maintain batch size 16, max length 256, early stopping patience 3. WHY: The initial plan's cosine annealing can cause learning rate spikes that harm fine-tuning; linear decay aligns with progressive unfreezing. Historical experiment #1 used this approach successfully. Higher weight decay and early stopping combat overfitting. HOW: In training loop, after each epoch, call function to unfreeze layers based on epoch. Create param groups with defined LRs. Use LambdaLR scheduler with decay factor (1 - epoch/num_epochs). Keep GradScaler and label smoothing. Early stopping patience remains 3."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
